  {
    "title": "Add a function with a unit test",
    "steps": [
      "Inspect the project layout and find the source and test files: `ls -la && find . -name \"*.py\" -not -path \"*/.git/*\" | head -50`",
      "Read the source module ({module}.py), its test file ({test_file}.py) and README.md to learn the existing naming, table and error conventions before writing anything",
      "Add the new code to {module}.py with the edit tool, reusing existing helpers/constants and the module's existing error class (e.g. UnitError) rather than inventing new ones; if the new behaviour is structurally different from the existing one (e.g. affine vs multiplicative), give it its own constant…",
      "Add a test class to {test_file}.py covering each supported case plus the error case, using the same style as the existing tests (unittest.TestCase, assertAlmostEqual/assertRaises)",
      "Run the full suite with `python3 -m unittest -v` and confirm all tests, old and new, pass"
    ]
  }
]

